Id: acc027w
Severity: WARN
Category: accounts
Check: check_accounts
Fix: chown the home directory to the account (chown -R user: ~user if the files in it are the account's too), or point the account at its own (usermod -d).
References: chown(1); usermod(8); CIS Ubuntu Linux 24.04 LTS Benchmark v2.0.0, 7.2.9 (Ensure local interactive user home directories are configured)
Controls: CIS 3.3, 5.1; NIST AC-2, AC-3; ISO 5.16, 5.18, 8.3; CE User access control

The home directory of the listed login ID is owned by another uid. The
owner of a home controls it: they can read and replace the shell's
start-up files, so commands run as the account at its next login, and
~/.ssh and authorized_keys, so they can log in as it (sshd's
StrictModes refuses a home or .ssh the user does not own, which is
one way this shows itself: key logins stop working). Homes end up
with the wrong owner when an account is renumbered, a home is restored
from elsewhere, or two accounts are pointed at one directory. CIS asks
that an interactive user own their home. The comparison is by uid, not
by name, so a second uid-0 account whose home is root's is not reported
for that; it is pass017w's. Root and the accounts from UID_MIN
(login.defs; else 1000, or 500 on macOS) up are judged, when they have
a login shell and a home that exists.
